{"name":"Bluff Stone","hex":"#d2bd9e","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bluff","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of bluffing; a false expression of the strength of one’s position in order to intimidate or deceive; braggadocio.","An attempt to represent oneself as holding a stronger hand than one actually does."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a bluff; to give the impression that one’s hand is stronger than it is.","To frighten, deter, or deceive with a false show of strength or confidence; to give a false impression of strength or temerity in order to intimidate or gain some advantage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bluff"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bluff (poker)","blocks":[{"text":"In the card game of poker, a bluff is a bet or raise made with a hand which is not thought to be the best hand. To bluff is to make such a bet. The objective of a bluff is to induce a fold by at least one opponent who holds a better hand. The size and frequency of a bluff determines its profitability to the bluffer. By extension, the phrase \"calling somebody's bluff\" is often used outside the context of poker to describe situations where one person demands that another proves a claim, or proves that they are not being deceptive."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Holdem.jpg/330px-Holdem.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bluff_(poker)","wikidata":"Q886106"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Bushnell_cartoon_about_Russia_and_Japan_bluffing_each_other.jpg/500px-Bushnell_cartoon_about_Russia_and_Japan_bluffing_each_other.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Bushnell_cartoon_about_Russia_and_Japan_bluffing_each_other.jpg/1280px-Bushnell_cartoon_about_Russia_and_Japan_bluffing_each_other.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Bushnell_cartoon_about_Russia_and_Japan_bluffing_each_other.jpg/500px-Bushnell_cartoon_about_Russia_and_Japan_bluffing_each_other.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In this 1904 cartoon by E. A. Bushnell, the Russian Empire (represented by a bear) and the Empire of Japan (represented by a fox) play poker, with their respective arsenals as stakes. Both wonder if the other is bluffing. The Russo-Japanese War began 17 days later.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABushnell_cartoon_about_Russia_and_Japan_bluffing_each_other.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg/1280px-Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A balancing rock called Kummakivi (literally \"strange stone\")[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/DirkvdM_rocks.jpg/500px-DirkvdM_rocks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/DirkvdM_rocks.jpg/500px-DirkvdM_rocks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rock outcrop along a mountain creek near Orosí, Costa Rica.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADirkvdM_rocks.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/GabbroRockCreek1.jpg/500px-GabbroRockCreek1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/GabbroRockCreek1.jpg/1280px-GabbroRockCreek1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/GabbroRockCreek1.jpg/500px-GabbroRockCreek1.jpg?utm_source=